This paper investigates the generalized Bi-Choquard equation on R^N, specifically the one given by \begin{align*}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\left(-\Delta\right)^2u=\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\left(\frac{u^{2}}{\left|x-y\right|^{ \beta}}\right)dy\right)u^{2p-1},\quad in\quad \mathbb{R}^{N},\\
u\geq 0,\quad in\quad \mathbb{R}^{N},
\end{array}
\right. \end{align*} where N=4 and the parameter $\beta$ is set to 2. Prior research is built upon to obtain multiple standardized solution standards under specific exponential norm conditions. A compact embedding operator is established to obtain the standard solution standard, while a compact embedding operator and a uniform boundedness criterion are established to obtain the standard solution standard.

This study investigates the formulation of the differential decay rate for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ reaction, considering form factors from several theoretical frameworks, including lattice QCD, Bjorken normal trajectory, saturable vertex, and quark model scenarios. By examining the influence of theoretical assumptions, this work identifies the degree to which experimental measurements can constrain form factor interpretations. Based on predicted outcomes from experimentally accessible regions, this study evaluates the formulation of the differential decay rate for $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay, finding that lattice QCD form factors lead to the most consistent interpretation of data across different theoretical frameworks. Additionally, this study determines the precision that can be achieved in measuring key physical properties, including the $B_s^0$ meson lifetime and semileptonic decay width, which can be highly accurate when form factors are well constrained. However, in cases where form factors are less well understood, similar experiments for $B^0ightarrow D^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay may be necessary to accurately determine these properties.
